@h1 Reuse: components & multi-file h1@

Name a piece of content once, use it by name anywhere, and change it in one place.
Hash defines, at-sign uses. This page covers your own nodes, captures, the
parameter forms at a glance, splitting a document across files, and the draft
workflow that ties it together.

@h2 Define your own node h2@

Three body shapes, from shortest to fullest. Each is a definition with a
@@@code # code@@@; each is recalled later with a bare @@@code @name code@@@.

A single-line value — a colon, then the value on one line:

@@@pre
#year: 1965

The bridge opened in @year and has not closed since.
pre@@@

That renders @strong The bridge opened in 1965 and has not closed since. strong@
Write the year once; every mention follows the definition.

A value block, for content that runs longer — a colon, then indented lines, then a
@@@code !! code@@@ fence:

@@@pre
#epigraph:
The river keeps no record of the boats.
— dock proverb
!!

@epigraph
pre@@@

The block form, for a wrapper whose body has a slot. Open with just the
hash-name, mark the slot with @@@code ... code@@@, close with the name and a hash.
The caller's body drops in where the dots are:

@@@pre
#note
@hr hr@
...
note#

@note
Deliveries pause for the harvest week.
note@
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 Definitions are hoisted, so a use may come before its
definition, and a definition body may reference another definition. aside@

@h2 Captures: parameters for your own node h2@

Declare parameters between double bars on the definition, drop each one into the
body with a @@@code ::field:: code@@@ wrapper, and pass them at the call:

@@@pre
#chapter ||number, title||
@h2 Chapter ::number:: — ::title:: h2@
...
chapter#

@chapter |number I| |title The Arrival|
The road climbed for an hour before the house appeared.
chapter@
pre@@@

The call supplies @code I code@ and @code The Arrival code@; the definition places
them in the heading and splices the body where the dots are. One template,
consistent chapters.

The capture list is optional — leave it off and Wit infers the parameters by
scanning the body for @@@code ::name:: code@@@ holes. A @@@code ::field:: code@@@
resolves @strong only inside a definition body strong@; in ordinary prose it
renders literally. To put a value into a sentence, define it and reference it by
name instead.

@h2 Parameter forms at a glance h2@

The same definition can be filled several ways. The governing rule:
@strong content goes in the body, metadata in parameters strong@. Pick by shape —
the full grammar is in the @a |href /docs/reference/syntax/| syntax reference a@.

@ul
@li @strong Pipes strong@ — @@@code |key value| code@@@; the only form that also
carries a body. li@
@li @strong Parens strong@ — @@@code @badge(tone good) code@@@; self-closing, good
for one or two inline params. li@
@li @strong Record strong@ — @@@code { key - value } code@@@ or
@@@code { key: value } code@@@; self-closing, structured, no body. li@
@li @strong Form-fill strong@ — the body @em is em@ the params, one
@@@code key: value code@@@ per line; best when there are many fields. li@
@li @strong Colon-scatter strong@ — @@@code key:value code@@@ tokens lifted from a
one-line body, for tagging a node mid-sentence. li@
ul@

Every value is type-probed, so a number, record, or collection passed through any
form arrives as a real typed value you can iterate or reach into.

@h2 Split a document across files h2@

A long work does not want one enormous file. Put shared definitions — a citation
format, a schema, recurring values — in their own file, and pull them in with a
@code reference code@ directive at the top of a line:

@@@pre
reference ./shared/sources.wit
reference ./shared/schema.wit
pre@@@

Everything defined in those files is now available here, as if typed in place. The
namespace is flat and definitions hoist, so import order does not matter.

@aside(class note) 💡 @code reference code@ is only special at the very start of a
line, followed by a path — which is why you only ever see it in a code block. In
running prose the same word is just a word. aside@

Wrap each chapter in a block definition so it emits nothing on its own, then let a
thin master file place the parts in order:

@@@pre
reference ./chapters/one.wit
reference ./chapters/two.wit

@chapter_one chapter_one@
@chapter_two chapter_two@
pre@@@

Reorder the uses and the chapters reorder; add a third and it slots in. The master
file is the table of contents made executable.

@h2 The draft workflow h2@

The source is plain @code .wit code@ text in a git repository, so every draft is a
readable diff — you see which sentences moved, not an opaque blob:

@@@pre
git commit -am "Tighten the opening; cut the second epigraph"
pre@@@

Tag or branch a draft the way you would any code, and rebuild to share:

@@@pre
wit build manuscript.wit -o draft.pdf
pre@@@

Run @code wit fmt manuscript.wit -w code@ before committing to re-indent to the
real structure and keep diffs clean; it parses the file first, so it doubles as a
sanity check.

